From bc21555dcee2c14964748309374e13cabfc20c03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DisplayXR <dev@displayxr.invalid>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 23:56:57 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(#164): score the geometric join against the
 candidate AS CLIPPED

A 3D tile inside an `overflow:auto` panel drops to raw side-by-side once
roughly 30% of it is scrolled out of the panel -- reported on an 8K/300% box,
and now reproduced on a 4K/250% one, which rules out the DPI reading the issue
opens with.

MEASURED, composition sample case 08, sweeping the inner scroller through
0/15/30/45/60% clipped (0.1.19 + patch 0119, Leia panel):

  inline-3D NO match for target=1407,766 950x420 among 39 root quads
    (best rejected score=532/1000, bar=700, clip=0,218 3840x1822)
  inline-3D ID-MATCH FALLBACK: ... target=1407,766 950x420 ... elsewhere=0
  inline-3D withheld 1 of 2 weave rects ...

`clip=0,218 3840x1822` is THE VIEWPORT. The panel's clip is nowhere in it.

THE ASYMMETRY. `RootResourceQuad::rect` is deliberately the UNCLIPPED quad
rect, while a tracked element rect has ALREADY been cut by its ancestor clip
chain -- patch 0111 (browser#130) does that in cc, on purpose, so the weave can
never paint outside a container. `overlap_score` then normalises the
intersection by `max(area)`, which is the unclipped area, so the ratio IS the
element's visible fraction and the 700 overlap bar silently becomes a
70%-VISIBILITY bar. Below it the tile matches nothing, is neither suppressed
nor woven, and the page draws its own side-by-side buffer.

That is browser#117's defect verbatim, returning through a door 0082 did not
close. 0082 made the comparison clip-symmetric via `common_clip_for`, but the
only clip a tracked rect carries is `root_target_clip` -- the SURFACE's
footprint, i.e. the embedding window. A nested panel's clip never reaches it,
so both sides get cut by the viewport and neither by the thing that actually
cut one of them.

THE FIX, from the other side, with a quantity the aggregator already has.
Record each candidate's OWN clip (the SharedQuadState's `clip_rect`, mapped to
root space) and cut the candidate with it before scoring. It is the SAME clip
chain cc applied to the tracked rect, so the two become comparable by
construction and a clipped tile scores ~1000 against its own quad at any
visibility.

Deliberately NOT the alternative: plumbing the ancestor clip from cc through
`TrackedElementRect`, the mojom and the traits. That is the same fact travelling
a second time, over thirty files, when it is already sitting on the quad.

`clip_rect` ONLY, never `visible_rect`. A clip is a layout fact both sides
share; visible_rect also carries OCCLUSION, which a tracked rect knows nothing
about -- scoring against it would break joins that work today, and browser#167
is precisely the case where a tile's visible_rect goes empty while its element
rect does not.

WHY IT CANNOT REGRESS A WORKING JOIN. Intersecting the candidate can only
shrink `max(area)`, so scores only ever RISE: no match that clears the bar
today can stop clearing it. Nor does it loosen the join -- the metric is still
intersection / max(area), so a small clipped quad sitting inside a large target
still scores its own area over the target's and is still rejected. The only
thing that stops being punished is the case that was never a mismatch: the same
element, cut.

The miss log gains `cands_with_clip=`. Zero there, next to a near-miss score,
would say cc folded the panel's clip into the quad geometry rather than onto
the SharedQuadState -- the one shape this fix cannot see, and the first thing
to check before guessing again.

Standing repro: composition case 08 in displayxr-web, which is where this was
measured.
---
 .../viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc | 55 ++++++++++++++++++-
 .../viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.h  | 23 ++++++++
 2 files changed, 76 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc b/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc
index 6ec9df2..7d3b9c1 100644
--- a/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc
+++ b/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc
@@ -1549,6 +1549,18 @@ void SurfaceAggregator::CopyQuadsToPass(
           // browser#131: stamp the recording position as this quad's
           // front-to-back index (see RootResourceQuad::order).
           const size_t quad_order = root_resource_quads_.size();
+          // browser#164: and record the quad's OWN clip in root space. The SQS
+          // clip_rect lives in the DEST PASS's target space, not in the quad's
+          // content space, so it maps with |transform_to_root_target| ALONE —
+          // no PreConcat of quad_to_target_transform, which would apply the
+          // quad's own placement a second time. (Same distinction
+          // ComputeInline3dPlaneSplit makes when it intersects a mapped quad
+          // rect with an unmapped clip_rect.)
+          std::optional<gfx::Rect> clip_in_root;
+          if (dest_sqs && dest_sqs->clip_rect) {
+            clip_in_root = cc::MathUtil::MapEnclosingClippedRect(
+                dest_pass->transform_to_root_target, *dest_sqs->clip_rect);
+          }
           root_resource_quads_.push_back(
               {quad_data.remapped_resource_id,
                cc::MathUtil::MapEnclosingClippedRect(to_root, quad->rect),
@@ -1557,7 +1569,8 @@ void SurfaceAggregator::CopyQuadsToPass(
                uv, dest_sqs ? dest_sqs->layer_id : 0u,
                dest_sqs ? dest_sqs->layer_namespace_id
                         : std::pair<uint32_t, uint32_t>(),
-               dest_pass->id, from_root_resolved_pass, quad_order});
+               dest_pass->id, from_root_resolved_pass, quad_order,
+               clip_in_root});
         }
       }
     }
@@ -2552,6 +2565,7 @@ AggregatedFrame SurfaceAggregator::Aggregate(
     // when exactly one candidate qualifies — the slow-scroll/steady case.
     int64_t best_score = -1;
     int64_t best_rejected = -1;
+    size_t clipped_cands = 0;
     for (const RootResourceQuad& cand : quads) {
       // browser#117: candidates recorded for DIAGNOSIS ONLY are not join
       // candidates. This restores the exact pool the recording condition used
@@ -2559,7 +2573,38 @@ AggregatedFrame SurfaceAggregator::Aggregate(
       if (!cand.from_root_resolved_pass) {
         continue;
       }
-      const gfx::Rect& qr = cand.rect;
+      // ---- (browser#164) SCORE THE CANDIDATE AS IT IS ACTUALLY CLIPPED ----
+      //
+      // |cand.rect| is the UNCLIPPED quad rect, while |target| is a tracked
+      // element rect that cc already cut with its ancestor clip chain
+      // (browser#130). Scoring one against the other normalises by the
+      // unclipped area, i.e. by the element's VISIBLE FRACTION — so the 700
+      // overlap bar silently becomes a 70%-VISIBILITY bar and a tile in an
+      // `overflow:auto` panel stops matching its own quad once ~30% of it is
+      // scrolled out. It is then neither suppressed nor woven and the page
+      // draws its raw side-by-side buffer. That is browser#117's defect
+      // exactly, returning through a door 0082 did not close: |clip| below is
+      // the SURFACE's footprint, and a nested panel's clip is not that.
+      //
+      // Measured on the composition sample's case 08, at 30% clipped:
+      //   NO match for target=1407,766 950x420 … best rejected score=532/1000,
+      //   bar=700, clip=0,218 3840x1822          <- the viewport, not the panel
+      //
+      // The candidate's own clip closes it from the other side, and it is the
+      // SAME clip chain cc applied to |target| — so the two are comparable by
+      // construction and a clipped tile scores ~1000 at any visibility.
+      //
+      // This can only RAISE scores, never lower them, so it cannot drop a
+      // match that works today. Nor does it loosen the join into false
+      // positives: the metric is still intersection / max(area), so a small
+      // clipped quad inside a big target still scores its own area over the
+      // target's and is still rejected. What it stops rewarding is the one
+      // case that was never a mismatch to begin with — the same element, cut.
+      gfx::Rect qr = cand.rect;
+      if (cand.clip_rect) {
+        ++clipped_cands;
+        qr.Intersect(*cand.clip_rect);
+      }
       if (qr.IsEmpty()) {
         continue;
       }
@@ -2593,6 +2638,12 @@ AggregatedFrame SurfaceAggregator::Aggregate(
                 << target.ToString() << " among " << quads.size()
                 << " root quads (best rejected score=" << best_rejected
                 << "/1000, bar=700, clip=" << clip.ToString()
+                // browser#164: how many candidates carried a clip of their own.
+                // Zero here, with a near-miss score, says cc folded the panel's
+                // clip into the quad geometry instead of onto the SQS — the one
+                // shape this fix cannot see, and worth knowing before guessing
+                // again.
+                << ", cands_with_clip=" << clipped_cands
                 << ") — overlay left to the page this frame";
       }
     }
diff --git a/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.h b/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.h
index 61bf468..7452846 100644
--- a/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.h
+++ b/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.h
@@ -547,6 +547,29 @@ class VIZ_SERVICE_EXPORT SurfaceAggregator : public SurfaceObserver {
     // order, smaller == closer to the viewer. Recorded rather than re-derived
     // because the joins below hand out a COPY of the entry, not its position.
     size_t order = 0;
+    // browser#164: the quad's OWN clip, in root space — the SharedQuadState's
+    // |clip_rect| mapped through the dest pass's transform_to_root_target.
+    // Nullopt when the quad carries no clip, which is the common case.
+    //
+    // The geometric join needs it because |rect| above is deliberately the
+    // UNCLIPPED quad rect while a tracked element rect is already cut by its
+    // ancestor clip chain (browser#130 does that in cc). Comparing the two
+    // directly normalises the intersection by the UNCLIPPED area, which is the
+    // element's visible fraction — the exact degeneration browser#117 was
+    // meant to end, returning through a door 0082 did not close: that patch
+    // made the comparison symmetric in the SURFACE's clip, and a nested
+    // `overflow:auto` panel's clip is not that. Cutting the candidate with its
+    // own clip restores the symmetry from the other side, using a quantity the
+    // aggregator already has, and it is the SAME clip chain cc applied to the
+    // tracked rect — so a clipped tile scores against its own quad at ~1000
+    // again, at any visibility.
+    //
+    // |clip_rect| ONLY, never |visible_rect|: a clip is a layout fact both
+    // sides share, while visible_rect also carries OCCLUSION, which the tracked
+    // rect knows nothing about. Scoring against an occluded quad's visible_rect
+    // would break the joins that work today (and browser#167 is precisely a
+    // tile whose visible_rect goes empty while its element rect does not).
+    std::optional<gfx::Rect> clip_rect;
   };
   std::vector<RootResourceQuad> root_resource_quads_;
   // browser#117: how many diagnostic-only (non-root-resolved-pass) entries are
